    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d459">

                <pb facs="0668" n="568" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg568"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 France/992</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-06-07">June 7, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The French Ambassador expressed the view that, as Barthou was to return to Paris
                    from Geneva on Friday for a Cabinet Meeting on Saturday, Undoubtedly the
                    position of the French Government with respect to debt payments would be taken
                    on that date; he said that the British note<anchor n="28" xml:id="d459-f1"/>
                    declining to make any payment on account made it far more difficult for the
                    French Government to make payment; the relations between Paris and London had to
                    be carefully considered and with the popular attitude in France similar to that
                    in England, it would be exceedingly difficult for the Government to adopt a
                    different course from that adopted by the British Government; the Ambassador
                    said that he hoped that his Government, even if it could make no part payment
                    now, would express the hope of early negotiations for a final <pb facs="0669"
                        n="569" xml:id="pg569"/> settlement and, on the opening of such negotiations
                    and as an earnest of France’s sincerity, that the Government would make a
                    substantial payment. This is the course he said he would recommend to Paris.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
